it's michelle from lab muffin beauty  science here chemistry phd and skincare  nerd  and person who hates washing their face  over the sink so today i thought i'd  show you how i remove my makeup and wash  my face  without using a sink while busting some  cleanser mitts  i already busted a whole bunch of myths  that people sent in but i couldn't  finish busting them all  i used to use this method a lot when i  was camping and on the plane and i've  been using this method in australia  because it's currently  winter here and i hate washing my face  at night and having the water drip down  my wrists and  into my sleeves and then i have to go to  sleep wearing wet sleeves  i'm going to start by removing my eye  makeup with a two-phase eye makeup  remover  i don't think i've ever talked about  this one before but it's the garnier  clean sensitive two-in-one gentle makeup  remover these two face removers have two  layers the blue layer is an oily layer  this one is based on cyclopentasiloxane  which is a silicone that's very light  and evaporates easily the bottom layer  is water-based and it's got lots of  humectants in it to hydrate i use it  with a reusable cotton velour pad this  one is from the brand bamboo  so with these you just shake it up first  and then you pour it onto the pad  i really like these two face makeup  removers because they take off  waterproof eye makeup  really gently and quickly without too  much tugging or irritation  micellar water works for this too but i  find that these ones are just a bit  quicker when i'm traveling i use  micellar water because it just means i  have one less thing to carry  i found that some of these two in one  makeup removers do leave a little bit of  an oily residue  it really depends on which one you're  using and where you're using it as well  this one doesn't leave too much of a  residue but it is still there i don't  mind that oily feeling near my eyes but  i really don't like it on other parts of  my face so if you don't like that oily  film or you're trying to put makeup on  top right away  then i would recommend a micellar water  instead okay let's get into the mix  again unfortunately a lot of these  answers aren't just a simple yes or no  that is actually really how a lot of  these myths came about there is some  grain of truth in there  first myth you need to use an oil-based  cleanser to remove makeup or sunscreen  because oil dissolves oil  you don't it really depends on how  strong your cleanser is how resistant  your sunscreen and makeup are  and really what you prefer to use in  your routine all cleanses weren't really  popular in skincare until pretty  recently and people got on fine without  it and that's even with things like  grease paint that you use for stage  makeup  if you think about washing greasy dishes  you don't need a cleansing oil to wash  those you just use dishwashing liquid  the reason is dishwashing liquid and  regular cleansers have  ingredients in them called surfactants  so factors look like this they have a  water loving head  and an oily tail that likes oil in the  one package  so that means the factors can help oil  droplets go into water and then they can  be washed off so that oil dissolves oil  thing still works because  that oily surfactant tail can basically  behave like an oil analysis skin  perspective on instagram she's a great  person to follow  she did this cool demonstration with a  uv camera after putting on a water  resistant sunscreen she washed one side  of her face with a regular gel cleanser  and the other side with a cleansing oil  followed by a gel cleanser  and you can see both sides ended up  around the same but a lot of people  including me still  really like using a cleansing oil or  balm first i like using very gentle  foaming cleansers and i like the feeling  of rubbing a balm  on my face it's really just a preference  so cleansing balm followed by a foaming  cleanser works best for me  now i'm moving on to a cleansing bun  this is then i met you living cleansing  bum this is one of my all-time favorite  products i actually got caroline hirons  onto it as well the next myth  is you need to use a water-based  cleanser after a cleansing oil to remove  the oil  this really depends on the cleansing oil  some of these leave more of a film  and others don't you can usually let the  oily residue stay on your face but some  people don't like the feel of that  that's the main reason i personally use  a water-based cleanser afterwards  sometimes i won't if i want to leave  that oil on my skin if my skin's feeling  really dry  i find that my skin tends to quite like  the oil but if your skin is breaking out  for example you might want to wash off  that oil and of course it really depends  on your skin and the particular oils in  your cleansing oil  when i normally cleanse at this stage i  would step into the shower and wash this  off but i'm not about to strip  and do that for you that is premium  content for a different website  so instead i'm going to be using a wet  cotton cloth to wipe this off this cloth  is from alpha h it comes in a set of  seven  it's really handy when i'm traveling  i'll sometimes use the makeup wipe for  this stage  so the next question is are makeup wipes  really bad  i guess it's a no makeup wipes aren't  that good for the environment and if  it's your only cleansing step then  it's probably not that good a lot of  makeup wipes just have water and a bit  of solvent in them they don't have  surfactants and so they're not going to  be as effective at removing  water resistant sunscreen and makeup as  something with surfactants  there are some wipes that use similar  ingredients to micellar water and those  will work a lot better  and apart from not removing sunscreen  and makeup properly the fact that they  don't work very well means that people  tend to  use them too much they use them too  harshly they put too much pressure on  they tug a lot at their skin  and that leads to irritation and if  you're using makeup wise every day  and that wipe is quite rough that can  also act as a physical exfoliation step  if your skin barrier isn't quite up to  daily physical exfoliation then that can  also irritate your skin there is also  the waist issue makeup wipes are often  made using a bit of plastic  so if you're using one a day it feels  kind of unnecessary  i only really use them in rare  situations where i don't have water and  a cloth handy  so makeup wipes aren't like liquid evil  but they're probably not the best option  for your daily cleanse  now on to my next step which is wiping  down with micellar water  i'm using biodermis sensor by h2o  micellar water this is my favorite i  talked about it a whole bunch  i'm using it with a terry cloth reusable  makeup pad this one is also from bamboo  so this is really just to get rid of  that extra oil from the cleansing balm  this particular cleansing balm leaves a  bit of residue now do you need to rinse  off micellar water i'm really sorry but  the answer is  it depends it mostly depends on the  micellar water you're using  because there are lots of different  micellar waters on the market they use  lots of different ingredients and they  have different formulas  if you're not sure about your micellar  water then it's probably best to rinse  it off but this particular one was  actually specifically developed for  people with sensitive skin  living in areas where the tap water can  be quite irritating so it was  specifically developed to avoid rinsing  and it was tested on thousands of people  with sensitive skin we often think of  surfactants as  irritating ingredients that need to be  rinsed off but the class of surfactants  is  really broad almost every skin care  product you use  has surfactants in it including all your  leave-on serums and moisturizers  one of the big reasons for this  confusion is that in regular chemistry  surfactants refer to  anything with a water-loving head and an  oil-loving tail but in cosmetic  chemistry a lot of the time people use  the word surfactant just to mean  the surfactants in traditional cleanses  i think the european commission  classifies them in quite a nice way they  divide them into different subclasses  one of them is cleansing but we also  have  dispersing emulsifying foam boosting  hydrotrope and solubilizing  so there are lots of different functions  that different surfactants can have  cleansing surfactants are the ones that  you find in cleanses while emulsifying  surfactants are the ones that are  used in traditional creams and  moisturizers and a lot of serums  and they keep the oily and watery parts  of those products together so they don't  separate  if you pick up any moisturizer chances  are it'll have saturated alcohol in it  that's a fatty alcohol  if we look it up in the european  commission database it's classified as  three types of surfactant  in any moisturizer there's going to be  some sort of emulsifying surfactant  it wouldn't be a moisturizer without  that ingredient so clearly some  surfactants can be left on your skin  without problems others can't  and of course it also depends on the  concentration and the formula the  surfactant that bioderma used in this  product  is peg six caprylic capri glycerides  this is classified as an emulsifying  surfactant  it isn't used as a cleansing surfactant  in regular cleanses  when it's included in a normal cleanser  it's actually used as a super fatting  agent these are ingredients that are  added to cleanses to make them more  gentle they act like oils or emollients  they hydrate and moisturize your skin  another micellar water i really like is  this one by bioessence  my friend larry year gave it to me when  i was in singapore last he is a makeup  artist  it actually uses that same surfactant  that's in the bioderma products  peg six caprylic capric glycerides it  also uses another one called peg 7  glycerol cocoa 8. unilever have also  presented research showing that their  micellar water is as gentle as regular  water  i'm assuming it's the simple one that  one also uses that same surfactant  i think there are definitely micellar  waters out there that use less gentle  surfactants and those should probably be  rinsed off  if your skin is having trouble with any  micellar water then just rinse it off  remember it could be something else in  the formula that's causing the  irritation maybe a fragrance or a  preservative or even just the ph of the  micellar water and it could also  just be the cotton pads that you're  using with your micellar water i found  that with rougher disposable makeup  pairs  my skin got really irritated with  micellar waters and that was just from  that extra physical exfoliation sort of  like if you use the makeup wipe every  day  these reusable cotton pads are a lot  softer than the disposable pads i was  using before and i haven't had a problem  with these and you can see from the pad  that i did actually have some makeup  left on my skin  after the cleansing balm it's not just  removing the oil do you need to cleanse  in the morning do you need to double  cleanse in the morning  well you do put products on your skin at  night if you're a skincare person  and on top of that overnight your skin  produces sebum and sweat  and dust probably falls on it as well so  there is definitely stuff on your face  that could be cleansed off but  in general your face is probably pretty  clean your products also get wiped off  onto your pillowcase  and in general it's really hard for any  products to form  a solid film on your skin that won't be  disrupted for a long time  there are actually studies where people  were trying to make barrier creams that  would prevent stuff from getting into  your skin  and even when they were trying it didn't  work very well  so it isn't like you've got a layer on  your face that other skincare products  can't get through by default most  studies on cleanses have been done on  people who have acne and the protocol  is always using the cleanser twice a day  i don't think there's actually any study  that's tested once versus twice a day  with cleanses  i personally found that not washing my  face with a proper cleanser in the  morning and just splashing water on my  face and wiping it off was  actually really beneficial i have very  dehydration prone skin that gets dried  out very easily with things like  cleanses  and i always get that tight  uncomfortable feeling on my face and i  also have oily skin and i found that  once i stopped using a proper cleanse in  the morning my face was a lot more  manageable i was actually producing less  oil  my skin was more hydrated but when i was  wearing masks a lot i found that my skin  broke out a lot more  and a quick wipe with a micellar water  in the morning was actually quite  helpful for that so i think this is a  very individual thing  it really depends on your skin and the  products you're using but it isn't a  hard and fast rule that you have to  cleanse a certain number of times a day  i'm sure we all know someone who never  washes their face properly and their  skin is really happy and never breaks  out  so all of this was really just an excuse  to multitask and clean my face while  working and so  i am ready for bed now let me know if  you try out this method like subscribe  click the notification bell check out my  blog and follow me on instagram i'll see  you next time good night

